From 31076f1bb82ab6b82fed7d7402829de8c72d4a88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vividh Yadav <178728541+VividhDesign@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:07:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Cost-based filter planner, flat label map, exact scan for
 small indexes

- Allow-filters use brute force over the allowed set when it is cheaper than
  the filtered graph walk: |allowed| <= sqrt(M0/2 * ef * n), calibrated with
  the new bench/bench_filter (the fixed 2048-label floor stays). 200k x 96-d,
  recall 1.0: 2% selectivity 125-316 -> 2,100-3,100 QPS; 5% 397-576 -> 1,430.
- The brute-force scan resolves labels with prefetching, sorts node ids and
  prefetches vectors (1.7-2.6x faster).
- The label -> node map is an open-addressing table (16 B/slot, 13.6 ns per
  prefetched lookup) instead of std::unordered_map.
- A query with ef >= live vectors scans exactly. Parallel construction can
  leave a node without inbound links (also in the original code: 2/3000 small
  builds in release, 110/3000 under ASan returned fewer than n results); this
  made a collection test flaky under ASan.
- Tried and dropped an ACORN-1 style two-hop filtered walk: slower at high
  selectivity and lower recall at low selectivity on this data.
- x86 benchmark results: GloVe-6B 100-d / 300-d and the memory benchmark.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHDAJht8Mhyh4r51AFKp8J
